ALVA — Redesign Settings Dialog with Top Section Navigation

Objective

Redesign ALVA’s existing Settings dialog to introduce a modern, organized, scalable tabbed section navigation system at the top of the dialog.

Currently, the Settings dialog contains several sections stacked vertically inside one scrollable container. Players must scroll down to find and access settings in lower sections.

This becomes increasingly inconvenient as more settings and categories are added.

The goal is to replace this long scrolling layout with separate, directly accessible settings sections, selected through a horizontal navigation bar at the top of the dialog.

Important: This is a structural UI/UX improvement, not a redesign of the actual settings or their functionality. Preserve ALVA’s established visual identity and existing behavior.

1. Understand the existing implementation

Before modifying anything, inspect the repository and understand how the game handles dialogs, input, localization, settings persistence, and responsive layouts.

Important files:

* js/ui/settings-dialog.js — Current dialog implementation and its three sections.
* css/settings.css — Styling for Settings, language selection, and the touch control editor.
* js/core/settings.js — Settings storage, validation, defaults, and persistence.
* js/core/menu-navigator.js — Shared keyboard and gamepad navigation.
* js/screens/discover-screen.js — Existing tabbed section navigation reference.
* css/discover.css — Existing tab styling and navigation patterns.
* js/localization/strings/en.js — English translations.
* js/localization/strings/fr.js — French translations.
* tests/interface/settings.test.mjs — Existing settings tests.
* ALVA_SPEC.md — Authoritative game specification, particularly §6.10.
* docs/development/conventions.md — Architectural and styling conventions.

Reuse established patterns where appropriate. Do not introduce a framework, dependency, or unnecessary architectural complexity.

2. Add top navigation tabs

The Settings dialog should have the following structure:

Header

ALVA / Settings title and the existing Close button.

Navigation bar

A horizontal row of clickable category tabs immediately beneath the header:

Language | Controls | Combat

Content area

A dedicated panel displaying only the settings belonging to the currently selected category.

The navigation bar must remain visible while interacting with settings. The player should never need to scroll through unrelated categories to reach another section.

Each tab must:

* Be an actual interactive button.
* Have a clear active state.
* Be accessible using mouse, touchscreen, keyboard, and gamepad.
* Open its corresponding section immediately.
* Keep the Settings dialog open when switching sections.
* Never trigger a page transition or affect gameplay.
* Display properly in English and French.

Do not implement this as anchor links that scroll to different positions in the same long page.

Only one settings section should be visible at a time.

3. Organize existing settings

Preserve all existing settings, descriptions, choices, and functionality.

Language tab

Contains:

* English
* Français

Retain the existing selection indicators and radio-group semantics.

Changing the language must immediately update the game interface, including the Settings dialog and tab labels.

The dialog should remain open, and the Language tab should remain selected.

Controls tab

Contains:

* Mobile Controls
* Joystick option
* Classic Buttons option
* Existing visual previews
* Default and Selected indicators
* Customize touch controls button
* Description of the currently selected layout
* Custom layout indicator when applicable

The Customize button must continue opening the existing touch layout editor.

Returning from the editor must restore the Settings dialog with the Controls tab still selected, preserving the appropriate focus.

Do not alter keyboard/gamepad mappings or change existing touch layout behavior.

Combat tab

Contains:

* Combat Assist
* Existing explanation
* On option
* Off option
* Default and selected indicators

Combat Assist must retain its current behavior, default value, and persistence.

Do not alter combat calculations, player mechanics, CPU behavior, or Watch Mode.

4. Visual design requirements

The new navigation must feel native to ALVA.

Preserve the existing:

* Dark translucent glass dialog
* Background dimming and blur
* Typography
* Green accent colors
* Border and corner styling
* Button styling
* Spacing conventions
* Focus indicators
* Overall aesthetic

Design the tab navigation as an integrated part of the modal rather than as a separate floating component.

Active tab

The selected tab should have:

* Stronger text contrast
* A visible green bottom indicator or underline
* A subtle accent background, if appropriate
* An unmistakable selected state, not communicated by color alone

Inactive tabs

Inactive tabs should have:

* Muted text
* Appropriate hover feedback
* Clear keyboard/gamepad focus indicators
* No distracting animations

Use subtle transitions consistent with ALVA’s existing animation timings.

Respect reduced-motion preferences.

Avoid excessive glow, oversized buttons, decorative gradients, or introducing a new visual language.

5. Layout and scrolling behavior

The layout should behave as three primary regions:

1. Fixed dialog header
2. Fixed top section navigation
3. Flexible active-section content area

The header and navigation tabs should remain visible at all times.

Only the selected section’s content may scroll vertically when it cannot fit in the available space.

Switching sections should immediately display the selected panel at its top, or restore its previous internal scroll position if implemented consistently.

Important: Do not allow the entire Settings dialog to become one large vertically scrolling collection of categories again.

Preserve the current behavior in which Home remains fixed behind the modal.

Use appropriate CSS grid/flex sizing, including min-height: 0 where necessary, to avoid overflow issues.

6. Responsive layout

ALVA supports desktop browsers, tablets, and mobile devices in landscape orientation. All must be supported.

Desktop

Display the category tabs in a horizontal row beneath the header.

Distribute spacing naturally and ensure the content area uses the available dialog space efficiently.

Mobile and compact landscape

The section navigation must remain horizontal and accessible, even when vertical screen space is limited.

Requirements:

* Keep tabs easy to tap, preferably with a minimum 44px interaction target.
* Prevent tab labels from overlapping or becoming unreadable.
* Use compact padding and typography where appropriate.
* If future categories exceed the available width, allow the tab bar itself to scroll horizontally.
* Do not shrink category names until they become illegible.
* Automatically reveal the selected tab if it sits outside the visible part of the horizontal navigation.
* Avoid introducing horizontal scrolling to the entire dialog.
* Keep the Close button accessible.
* Ensure the active settings panel remains usable in very short landscape viewports.

Retain existing compact-mode behavior where appropriate, including hiding optional control-card descriptions below the existing height threshold.

Do not hide the Combat Assist explanation merely to make the panel smaller.

7. Keyboard and gamepad navigation

ALVA already has a shared MenuNavigator system.

Integrate the new tabs with this system rather than building a competing input handler.

Expected behavior:

* Mouse click or touch selects a tab.
* Keyboard and gamepad users can reach the navigation tabs.
* Left/Right arrows and gamepad D-pad can navigate between category tabs.
* The current category updates when a tab is activated.
* Players can move naturally between the navigation bar and the settings within the active section.
* Confirm inputs continue activating the focused setting.
* Navigation must never reach hidden sections.
* Navigation must never escape to the Home screen while Settings is open.

Follow the existing Discover screen’s tab-navigation conventions where appropriate.

Consider using the dialog’s navigation scope and its directional input handling for predictable tab movement.

Important: ALVA’s shared navigator focuses elements on mouse hover by default. Tab hover must not accidentally change the selected section.

Use the established data-nav-no-hover-focus convention or equivalent appropriate handling.

When switching tabs, make sure focus remains valid. Do not leave keyboard or gamepad focus on an element that has become hidden.

Opening Settings

Open on the Language section by default.

Focus the active Language tab so the category navigation is immediately discoverable and accessible.

Closing Settings

Preserve every current dismissal method:

* Close button
* Escape
* Gamepad Back
* Clicking the dimmed backdrop

Return focus to the Home settings gear.

Make sure the dialog’s navigation scope is removed exactly once.

8. Accessibility

Implement proper tabbed-interface semantics.

Use:

* role="tablist" on the category navigation
* role="tab" on category buttons
* role="tabpanel" on category content panels
* aria-selected for the current tab
* aria-controls to connect tabs to panels
* aria-labelledby to connect panels with their tabs
* Roving tabindex where appropriate
* Correct hidden states for inactive panels

Only the selected tab should participate in normal Tab navigation, while directional navigation must still reach the other tabs.

Ensure inactive panels are genuinely hidden from rendering, assistive technologies, and input navigation. Add explicit CSS for [hidden] panels where necessary so existing display rules cannot override hiding.

Preserve the existing modal semantics:

* role="dialog"
* aria-modal="true"
* Dialog title association
* Focus restoration
* Background inert behavior

Do not break the radio groups within individual sections.

The category tabs represent navigation, while radio controls represent saved setting choices. Keep these concepts semantically separate.

9. Scalable architecture for future settings

This is a major reason for the change.

ALVA will receive more settings and categories over time.

Make it straightforward to add another settings section without rewriting the dialog’s layout or navigation logic.

Prefer a small, centralized section definition containing information such as:

* Stable section ID
* Localization key
* Section content or content builder

Generate the navigation tabs and corresponding panels from this definition where practical.

Keep category ordering predictable.

Avoid hardcoding navigation behavior specifically for exactly three tabs.

Adding a fourth or fifth category should not require changing the navigation algorithm, layout structure, or overflow system.

However, do not introduce speculative or empty settings categories now.

Only the three existing categories should appear in this update.

Do not change the saved settings schema simply to support UI navigation.

The selected tab is a presentation concern, not a new gameplay preference.

10. Preserve all existing functionality

This implementation must not change how settings are saved or applied.

Preserve:

* Immediate settings persistence
* Existing localStorage key and schema version
* Saved language behavior
* Saved mobile controls behavior
* Per-layout custom touch configurations
* Combat Assist behavior
* First-launch language selection
* English/French localization
* Touch layout editor integration
* Existing menu and gamepad input mappings
* Home screen behavior
* Dialog opening and closing behavior

Do not modify gameplay code unless a genuine integration issue requires it.

Avoid unrelated refactoring.

11. Tests and verification

Update and extend the existing tests in tests/interface/settings.test.mjs.

At minimum, verify:

1. Exactly three category tabs exist: Language, Controls, and Combat.
2. Language is selected by default when Settings opens.
3. Exactly one panel is visible at any moment.
4. Selecting Controls hides Language and Combat.
5. Selecting Combat shows only Combat settings.
6. Changing settings still saves the correct values.
7. Switching categories does not reset any settings.
8. Hidden section controls cannot be reached through the menu navigator.
9. Keyboard and gamepad directional navigation works across tabs.
10. Focus remains inside the dialog.
11. Opening and closing the touch editor returns to Controls.
12. Switching languages updates tab labels and settings text immediately.
13. Closing Settings restores focus to the Home gear.
14. Reopening Settings has the specified default selection.
15. The active tab has correct ARIA attributes.
16. Adding an additional section to the section configuration would work without rewriting navigation.

Update existing assertions that assume all three sections are simultaneously visible.

Preserve unrelated test coverage.

Run:

node --test tests/interface/settings.test.mjs

Then run the complete test suite:

node --test

Perform real-browser visual verification because the fake DOM tests cannot validate layout geometry, focus visibility, horizontal overflow, or touch responsiveness.

Test desktop, compact landscape mobile, narrow screens, and French translations.

12. Documentation

Update ALVA_SPEC.md, particularly section 6.10, to accurately describe the new Settings navigation.

Document:

* Top tab navigation
* One visible section at a time
* Focus and directional navigation rules
* Internal scrolling behavior
* Responsive horizontal tab overflow
* Future section extensibility

Preserve the specification’s existing descriptions of settings and their behavior.

Update other relevant documentation only where necessary.

13. Acceptance criteria

The implementation is complete when:

* Players can open Settings and immediately see Language, Controls, and Combat as clickable tabs at the top.
* Switching between these categories requires no vertical scrolling.
* Each section contains only its corresponding settings.
* The header and category navigation remain accessible while scrolling section content.
* The layout looks consistent with ALVA’s current visual design.
* The interface works on desktop, mobile, keyboard, touchscreen, and gamepad.
* English and French work correctly.
* Existing settings, their persistence, and the touch editor behave exactly as before.
* The system is designed to accommodate additional settings categories.
* All relevant tests pass and documentation matches the implementation.

Final instructions

Implement the changes directly in the existing architecture.

Prioritize simplicity, responsiveness, maintainability, accessibility, and consistency with the rest of ALVA.

Do not create a separate Settings screen, replace the existing modal, introduce a framework, or redesign unrelated interfaces.

The central principle is simple: Settings should be organized into immediately accessible categories, not one long page that players have to scroll through.

After implementation, provide a concise summary of changed files, resulting behavior, test results, and any remaining limitations.
